    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.minimum_spanning_arborescence -\n    EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.minimum_spanning_arborescence.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.minimum_spanning_arborescence()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Find a minimum-weight spanning arborescence of a directed graph (Edmonds'\n    algorithm).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.minimum_spanning_arborescence()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Find a minimum-weight spanning arborescence of a directed graph (Edmonds'\n    algorithm).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.minimum_spanning_arborescence()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n  .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Find a minimum-weight spanning arborescence of a directed graph (Edmonds'\n  algorithm).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind a minimum-weight spanning arborescence of a directed graph (Edmonds' algorithm).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L87176-L87278){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.minimum_spanning_arborescence(\n    weight_attr=None,\n    include=None,\n    exclude=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Minimum spanning arborescence](../assets/graphs/minimum-spanning-arborescence.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"One-way links between four relay stations, weighted by cost: A→B (4), A→C (1), C→B (2), B→D (3), C→D (6), and D→C (1). The cheapest tree reaching every station from the root A, outlined in the accent color, uses A→C, C→B, and B→D, in the accent color; the other links are faded. The card lists the three edges and their total cost 1 + 2 + 3 = 6.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA spanning arborescence is a directed tree in which a single root reaches every node:\nthe root has no incoming edge and every other node has exactly one. This method returns\nthe cheapest one over every possible root. It is the directed counterpart of the\nminimum spanning tree, and answers questions like \"what is the cheapest way to\nbroadcast from one site to all others over one-way links?\". Edge direction matters:\nan edge `u -> v` can only carry the signal from `u` to `v`.\n\nWith `include=` and `exclude=`, the result is the cheapest spanning arborescence that\nuses every included edge and none of the excluded ones; `iter_spanning_arborescences()`\nbuilds on this to list the arborescences in order of cost. Self-loops are never chosen,\nand the original graph is not modified.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[weight_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Name of the edge attribute holding the weights. If `None` (the default), each edge's\n    weight field is used (`1.0` on an unweighted graph), as it is for `\"weight\"`. Under any\n    other name, every edge must hold a number.\n\n<code>[include]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Edges the result must contain, as edge IDs or `(u, v)` node pairs (a pair stands for\n    every edge from `u` to `v`).\n\n<code>[exclude]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Edges the result must not contain, given the same way.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new directed graph with every node of the original (IDs, labels and attributes\n    kept) and the `n - 1` arborescence edges (weights and attributes copied).\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is undirected, an edge value is NaN, `weight_attr=` names an attribute that\n    an edge lacks or holds as a non-number, no spanning arborescence exists (no single node\n    can reach all the others), or none uses every included edge and avoids every excluded\n    one.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThis is the Chu-Liu/Edmonds algorithm: Edmonds' cycle contraction on the negated\nweights, with the edge count ranked first so that every node gets a parent except the\nroot (see `maximum_spanning_arborescence()`). It runs in $O(V E)$ time. Ties between\nequally cheap arborescences may be broken differently than in NetworkX.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~maximum_spanning_arborescence`) \n\n:   The arborescence with the largest total weight.\n\n[](`~iter_spanning_arborescences`) \n\n:   The arborescences in order of weight, beyond the cheapest.\n\n[](`~directed_steiner_tree`) \n\n:   A cheap arborescence that reaches only some of the nodes.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\n`B` can be reached straight from the root `A`, or by way of `C`. The direct edge is\ncheaper, so the arborescence takes it:\n\n::: {#ef988b21 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\", 1.0), (\"A\", \"C\", 2.0), (\"C\", \"B\", 3.0)], directed=True\n)\ngraph.minimum_spanning_arborescence().get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['A->B', 'A->C']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### The cheapest pipes to keep running\n\n`pipeline()` is a made-up city's water supply, with each pipe's running `cost`. Keeping\nwater flowing from the Reservoir to every station needs one pipe into each of the other\nten, and the cheapest choice costs 13:\n\n::: {#dd8d4024 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npipeline = ec.pipeline()\ncheapest = pipeline.minimum_spanning_arborescence(weight_attr=\"cost\")\n\ncheapest.count_edges(), cheapest.get_edge_df()[\"cost\"].sum()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(10, 13)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#a1ea006b .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npipeline.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\",\n    highlight_edges=cheapest.get_edge_ids(),\n    edge_label_by=\"cost\",\n    label_by=\"label\",\n    width=700,\n    height=360,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade0